WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.350
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.900
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliffe,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.780
I'm the manager of the
student arts programs at MIT

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.370
and we're all here today to
kick off the 2020-2021 15K

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.870
creative arts competition.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.320
I'm really excited to
introduce everybody.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.220
We have team Elements
who are 15K first place

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.330
winners last year.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.100
We've got Michael
and Maria, I've

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.300
Ellen Shakespear from 2018,
her venture is club Spaceus,

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.870
and then I've got fantastic
mentors, Nir Hindi

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.300
and John Corbiere
also joining to offer

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.100
some of their
perspectives about what

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.460
it's like to help these ventures
facilitate their success.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.110
Let's start by going around.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.650
Everybody give me a minute
long intro and a brief overview

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:45.460
of your venture.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.240
We'll start with Elements.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.300
Hi, everyone.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.220
I'm Maria, I'm an
architect by training

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.020
and I recently
graduated from MIT,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.690
from a master's of science
in architecture studies

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.550
and I'm co-founder of Elements.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.120
So Elements is design
learning in a box,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.090
we're developing at home
art and design curriculum

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.310
for students ages 10 to 14.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.960
And we're hoping
to expand access

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.870
to design education by
putting creative curriculum

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.330
in the hands of students
around the world.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.470
And beyond the
co-founder is Michael.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:18.985
Everyone.

00:01:18.985 --> 00:01:20.360
It's good to see
you this Sunday,

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.430
my name is Michael Stradley, I'm
also an architect by training,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.660
I just finished a master
in science courses

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.230
in architectural design at MIT.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.550
And the way Elements started,
Maria and I were working down

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:38.060
in the fall of 2019 and we were
just teaching a small design

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.190
seminar, looking at
experimental teaching

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:45.440
methods in architecture
and the idea felt strong,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.330
our partnership felt
strong, and so we

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:51.560
built that kind of rush
collaboration into Elements.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.380
We've been working
on that ever since.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.010
Excellent.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.930
Thank you both for
that introduction.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:00.020
Why don't we pop
over to Ellen next.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.990
Thanks Shannon, And thanks
for the invitation to be here.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.940
It's a pleasure.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.250
My name is Ellen and about
three years ago my co-founder

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.160
Stephanie and I
started our venture

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.140
which is called Spacers.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.520
And Spacers works with
artists and property owners

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.940
to turn underused urban
space, whether that's

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.150
a vacant storefront
or perhaps a neglected

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.390
park in a neighborhood
into spaces for communities

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.060
to come together and for artists
and their work to be showcased.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.780
And we do this because
we believe that when

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.890
artists have space in the
city everybody benefits.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.540
And Stephy and I we started
it as architecture students

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.790
at MIT.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.700
It was as simple as walking down
Mass Ave. on the way to studio

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.910
every morning and seeing
these vacant storefronts along

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.580
in Central Square and
Kendall, and thinking

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.030
there's got to be a better
way to use these spaces

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:00.170
and simultaneously knowing that
the city of Boston and cities

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.140
elsewhere continuously
need space for artists.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.690
And so for the last
three and half years

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:09.170
we've been working on doing
that matching these spaces

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.180
with artists and
small businesses,

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.050
and entrepreneurs who need
the space to grow and thrive

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.460
in the city.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:21.680
Obviously that's a strange thing
to be working on during COVID.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.240
This idea of bringing
people together

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.330
indoors is something that
makes everybody kind of cringe

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:26.930
these days.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:31.100
Rightly so, recently we've been
expanding the kinds of places

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.130
that we work as well.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.390
So working outdoors with
communities and parks,

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.540
and parking lots even
this past summer we

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.790
put together a fare
for the city of Boston

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.790
that used to be more socially
distant and safe sites

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.090
to continue to
celebrate local art.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:47.900
That's excellent.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:48.802
Thank you so much.

00:03:48.802 --> 00:03:51.260
I also look forward to hearing
a little bit more about that

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.730
as we continue with the panel.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.120
Next up we have Neil Hindi.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:56.940
Everyone.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.940
Good afternoon
from Madrid, Spain,

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.360
my name is Neil coming
from original EO,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.220
background in the
entrepreneurial,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.380
business entrepreneurial.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.550
The background
started two companies.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.660
Today I always say
I kind of combine

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.830
two of my greatest passion,
health and entrepreneurship.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.170
I'm the founder of
the Alpian, and what

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.060
I do with the
Alpian is basically

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:20.720
I take art as a way of
thinking because for me art

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.460
is always about the mindset.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.050
It's not about the object.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.470
And I help business
organization to bring art

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.840
more as a way of thinking
into specifically

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.880
the innovation, processes
in business organization.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.120
And thanks Shannon for
inviting me to be here.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.135
It's a pleasure having you here.

00:04:40.135 --> 00:04:43.265
And next up we'll have our
last introduction from John.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:47.452
Hi, everyone.

00:04:47.452 --> 00:04:48.660
Thanks so much for having me.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:49.702
My name is John Corbiere.

00:04:49.702 --> 00:04:53.160
I run a animation and
design Studio in Toronto,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.670
Canada called Thought
Cafe, as well as

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:59.300
a technology experiential
company called Future Talk.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.930
We focus primarily
on factual content

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.860
course-based information
spreading essentially

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.100
and really, I've been
pushing the boundaries

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:13.250
in how we use augmented
reality and virtual reality

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.670
technologies to spread important
messages and help people,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.680
especially in the
compassion realm understand

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.120
social and political issues.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:24.680
Excellent.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.720
Thank you all so much.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.600
Now we're going to get into a
slightly deeper dive about each

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.580
of your ventures
and experiences,

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.370
and how they've related
to different aspects

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.660
that we try to
promote in the 15K.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.420
So first up for
Michael and Maria,

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:42.710
I would love to hear an update
about your progress since May

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.530
and if you can discuss
things like the ramp project

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.380
that we did with arts at MIT,
and how your testing is going.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.440
That would be great.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:50.940
Sure.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:51.720
Absolutely.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:57.210
And May feels like a lifetime
ago but since then we've

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.260
kept ourselves pretty busy.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:01.200
We just concluded
this program that you

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.760
referenced called RAMP.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.290
Like all good
things at MIT, it's

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.380
an acronym that stands for
Remote Art-Making Pilot.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.130
And so this is a six week kind
of experimental art and design

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.550
studio that Elements ran in
collaboration with the MIT

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.380
student art association.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.350
So just following
the 15K competition,

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.630
a student art association
reached out to us.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.110
They had the same problem is
everyone is teaching right now,

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.265
we need to do this
remotely, we won our support

00:06:27.265 --> 00:06:30.990
from the robust student
art community at MIT.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.420
How do we do this when we're
all distributed over the summer?

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.440
So we work closely
with them and we

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.660
developed this six
week pilot program that

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.480
was kind of unglamorous
and involved packing up

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.670
boxes with art
materials, shipping them

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.710
to people all
around the country,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.100
and then meeting once a week
for a few hours to discuss

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.640
and critique one
another's creative work.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.820
The goal of ramp for
us in addition to

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.470
doing some meaningful
teaching was

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:02.250
that it gave us the opportunity
to pilot a few to prototype

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.130
ideas that we have,
and get user feedback

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.530
from both undergraduate and
graduate students at MIT.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.390
So we-- specifically we tested
three pieces of curriculum

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.880
that we hope to develop
over the coming 12 months,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.030
those are going to become
products for live release.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:21.660
The first was curriculum about
color material and pigment

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.730
formulation that people
are taking objects

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.920
from the world turning
them into paint,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.420
the second was a kit that dealt
with cyanotype and photography

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.100
without the camera,
and the third

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.280
was a curriculum that looked
at rule-based and generative

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.080
drawing systems.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.660
Yes, [INAUDIBLE] we're
working on building

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.610
a prelaunch
prototype, which will

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.920
be made available to
a limited audience

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.850
this winter, so it's one of the
kites that Michael mentioned.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:51.120
It's the cyanotype and
photographic experiment kite,

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.140
and it explores the historical
photographic technique

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:58.500
of cyanotype, which allows
students to produce photographs

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.660
without a camera,
using only sunlight.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:05.400
And this kite will include
light sensitive cyanotype sheets

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.320
as well as a series
of design challenges

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.050
and historical reference,
and a range of materials

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.030
that will help the
students explore

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.650
and experiment with
this technique.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:16.560
Sounds fantastic--

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.920
If that sounds-- I have to
throw that this shameless plug

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.010
if this sounds compelling
to you or every young person

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.860
in your family or in
your kind of orbit.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.470
We hope to get these
available before the holidays.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:29.940
So we'll put the
link in the chat.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.482
If you sign up with
your email, we'll

00:08:31.482 --> 00:08:34.740
let you know just as soon as
we're finished working on it.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:35.850
Excellent.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:39.809
I'm also-- I neglected to
introduce my fantastic co-host

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:40.932
whose name is Theo Fields.

00:08:40.932 --> 00:08:43.140
I introduced him in the
beginning of the last session

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.600
but I know that we've had
some new people join us.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.940
So Theo is my
co-moderator and is also

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:51.480
going to be asking some
questions to everybody.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.910
Yes, Thanks for the warm intro.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:58.390
Some questions for Ellen,
a.k.a the Spacers team here.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:01.140
So you mentioned having
outposts in New York City

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.660
and also kind of a move
to pivot your business

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.690
model a little bit, away
from indoor spaces more

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.922
towards outdoor spaces,
safe collective gathering.

00:09:09.922 --> 00:09:11.880
Who have been your kind
of key partners in that

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.070
and what have been the
steps that have made

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:15.450
that successful and viable?

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:22.860
I think you are on mute cell.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:23.490
Yes.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.290
Classic.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.840
Thanks for the
thoughtful question.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.450
I think creative reframe
earlier mentioned

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.090
that nobody does this
type of work alone

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.240
and that is very much
true in our case.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.880
There's a constellation of
partnerships and resources

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.290
we rely on every day.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.980
I mean, not the least of which
are folks that you mentor

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.800
or that you meet rather
through organizations

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.240
and competitions like this.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:50.730
Truly we are still
in touch with mentors

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:53.310
that we met through
this very competition,

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:59.550
we email them constantly
for big asks and little asks

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:04.290
and so there's mentors that we
keep in touch with who provide

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.180
us with resources and insight.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.480
There are folks in the city
that have been particularly

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.160
essential for us working in the
real world in physical space

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.830
often requires us to think
about things like permitting,

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:21.070
licensing, how high can a
banner be et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:23.730
And we found a lot
of great resources

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.820
even just right in Boston.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.890
I suppose I'll give
a quick shout out

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.680
to a group called the mayor's
office of new urban mechanics,

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.880
a division of the mayor's office
that is specifically focused

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:39.210
on R&amp;D in the city
of Boston, perhaps

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.490
applicable to many teams.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:43.770
These are folks who are
really interested in testing

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:46.442
and experimenting in the city
of Boston and best of all

00:10:46.442 --> 00:10:48.150
they know exactly who
to put you in touch

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:51.570
with if there's a specific
division of the city that

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.530
might be applicable to
what you're working on.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:55.620
So they've been a
huge resource for us

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.710
and then I think lastly
and maybe most importantly,

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.600
our customers.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:03.090
We work with landlords
and we work with artists

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.720
and talking to them
constantly has provided us

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.080
with the kind of
invaluable insight

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.970
about what our next
step should be.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.220
It's Stephanie and I
working full time at Spacers

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:19.200
but we could go around in
circles for hours, validating

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.040
one move or the other.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:23.760
And then questioning
ourselves but the clarity

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.590
that we find when we
just pick up the phone

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.578
and talk to somebody who's
known us from the beginning, who

00:11:28.578 --> 00:11:30.120
we've worked with
from the beginning,

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.520
and who is out
there in the world

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:36.480
experiencing a reality
that is unique to them

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.120
and you unique to them
as a customer of ours,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.400
that has been illuminating.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.980
So whether it's through surveys
or just picking up the phone

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.230
and talking to people,
are you comfortable

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.937
going inside of spaces yet?

00:11:47.937 --> 00:11:49.770
Know like what does
that mean what would you

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:51.450
be comfortable with?

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:56.070
And just having a landscape of
folks that you can call and get

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:58.290
their feedback on has
been essential for us

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.740
as we both continue
to operate in Boston

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:03.870
and look elsewhere as well.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:04.440
Fantastic.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.357
I think there's a great
tick way for students,

00:12:06.357 --> 00:12:09.720
which is real customer research,
real customer conversations.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.790
This is crucial.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.560
Speaking of students in the
competition, Hindi and John,

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.220
you guys have both
mentored students

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.170
in this competition before.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.230
We were curious how
kind of fully developed

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.885
are some of the ideas when
you first encounter them

00:12:24.885 --> 00:12:26.760
or when you first
encounter the student teams

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.930
and what is the most impactful
in helping them kind of refine,

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:36.560
reform those ideas as they
go through the competition?

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:40.840
John you want to go first
or you want me to go?

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:43.030
Why don't you go ahead
first, I'll go right after.

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:44.935
OK, great.

00:12:44.935 --> 00:12:46.060
So I'll tell you something.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.190
I mean, we keep
mentioning it but I

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:50.560
think one of the most
important thing is the team

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.190
and I always say that I prefer
an A team with the B idea,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.870
than a B team with an A idea.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:57.850
Because the A team
will know how to turn

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.700
around an idea and
the nature of startup

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:03.080
is that you change
on a constant basis.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:07.720
So you have to be agile
and flexible in changing.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:11.230
In other things
that kind of what

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.120
I try to do at
least with the teams

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.940
that I mentor is that
to really push them

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.040
into the validation phase.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.410
Often we have
assumption in a startup

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:22.447
is basically a
bunch of assumptions

00:13:22.447 --> 00:13:23.530
that you need to validate.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.260
And I always encourage
them to leave the building.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:28.540
What they say don't
stick to the sloven.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.220
I think and I just mention it.

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:34.420
Having meaningful
conversations with a customer,

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.210
a professional customer
is super, super important

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:39.280
in order to validate a problem.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:41.620
Because in the end
I tell them, you

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.510
don't want to find
yourself in SIS.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.540
What we call a solution
in search of a problem.

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:49.150
You want to really make
sure that not only did you

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.190
have idea, that you have
an idea for the problem

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.830
that you're actually discover.

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:56.080
And I think that the
teams that actually

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:00.490
go outside of the comfort
zone and go outside

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.650
from the building and do
a speaking to customers.

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:07.330
Those are the ones that actually
able to make the progress

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:10.900
in the competition.

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:15.340
In the end, again, what I
try to do as a mentor is not

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:18.040
to provide an answer but
rather asking questions

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:21.580
to guide them through questions,
and challenge what they say

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.170
or challenge what they think.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:26.140
And one of the exercises
that I ask my teams to do

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:29.320
is to actually think
about everything

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:31.240
that can go wrong
with your startup,

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.990
and try to think how are
you going to tackle it.

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.740
Because in the world
of startup everything

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:39.380
that can go wrong most
likely will go wrong.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:42.764
So that's kind of
my perspective.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:47.700
That was fantastic.

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:53.170
I'll give it a little shot.

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:56.945
The thing is well, I think one
of the most important things

00:14:56.945 --> 00:14:58.570
you guys are coming
up with these ideas

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:01.480
is to share it and share
it with a lot of people.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:05.140
That's we get really caught
up with holding ideas really

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:07.883
close to our hearts, and being
afraid that if we share it

00:15:07.883 --> 00:15:09.550
with someone they
might take it from us.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.950
So they might do it
quicker, but the reality

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:16.210
is there's probably
dozens, if not hundreds

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:18.370
of people that are coming
to the same conclusions

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.260
that you're coming
to at the same time.

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:24.760
And the only way
to win this race

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:26.770
is to do it as a
relay with a team

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.110
and to be really, really
confident about that team

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:31.000
but to not hold things in.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.650
So test with as many
people as possible,

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:36.340
talk to people about
it, it's still going

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:40.120
to be your idea and your baby.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.910
Try to solve a common
problem, a simple problem, not

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:46.690
a super complex one,
especially for this competition

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:48.905
because it's going
to be very difficult,

00:15:48.905 --> 00:15:50.530
you're going to run
out of time and you

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:53.020
want something that is
simple but iterative

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:55.540
that you can really iterate on.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:58.660
Think about areas that
give people anxieties.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.240
How you can solve those
things, that kind of stuff

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:03.970
is becoming really,
really important right now

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:06.630
in the environment that
we're finding ourselves in.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.320
So I think you guys should
really focus on that.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:11.920
And then don't necessarily
focus on something

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:14.047
that you're good at.

00:16:14.047 --> 00:16:15.880
Obviously you should
do something in an area

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:18.820
that you're good at but
it's too easy to be sort

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:21.640
of to get tunnel
vision, to be caught up

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:23.740
in this great idea
about something

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.210
that you already
know a lot about,

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.130
something that you're
already good at,

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:31.540
but generally that's not always
going to end up being the best

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.640
idea that you may be
able to come up with.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.970
Sometimes the idea you least
expect it will come up.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:39.700
So you have to be willing
to have a lot of ideas,

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:41.830
be willing to throw
them out quickly

00:16:41.830 --> 00:16:46.120
and iterate and let
the process guide you

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:48.010
like an ocean swell
would guide you.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:50.890
Control your ship,
set a destination

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:54.280
but know that the pathway there
isn't going to necessarily

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:57.290
be exactly what you expected.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:00.100
So yeah, avoid tunnel
vision at all costs.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:04.319
And that's what I have
to say about that.

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:05.880
These are all
fantastic insights.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:07.950
This is great advice.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.890
In the spirit of keeping
things fresh and lively

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:15.270
and again not succumbing
to Zoom fatigue,

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:19.470
I want to take a few minutes now
and do a full group lightning

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:20.640
round.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.250
So we'll do two
minutes roughly each

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:28.050
and I'd like you all to tell us
what is one key piece of advice

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:30.210
or pearl of wisdom
that you would

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.630
give to prospective competitors
in this competition.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:34.755
Let's go with Elements.

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:37.750
OK.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:39.280
I can start.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:42.190
So for me the one
key piece of advice

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:44.950
would be forming a team
that shares the same vision

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:47.230
and the same values as you do.

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:50.080
I think the team defines
the culture of the startup,

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:53.020
and that's why working with
people whose beliefs and values

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:55.960
align to yours is
so important to make

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:57.460
your ventures successful.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:17:59.500
And I mean successful
not only in terms

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:02.200
of launching and
raising capital,

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.930
but also successful
at a personal level.

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:09.850
I think meaning that something
that is meaningful to you

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:13.610
and that you believe is
adding value to society.

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.090
So I think that would be--

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:18.430
also someone who you
do enjoy working with.

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.680
Because you're going to spend
a lot of time with them.

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:23.650
I think that would be mine.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:25.950
Yeah, absolutely.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.630
Obvious I agree
with all of that.

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:32.030
My advice, again, we're
very early [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.210
early on, so maybe don't
put too much stock in this

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:37.210
but I hope this is similar
to Jonathan's point, which

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:41.310
is you kind of can't be
precious with your idea

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.840
or what you're developing,
and my advice would

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:48.280
be to test the thing
as quickly as you can

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:49.930
and as often as you can.

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.120
Before Maria and I even--

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.180
before we had a name,
before we had any--

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:57.020
we were having
conversations about venture

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:59.500
with this startup
that, we were testing

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.080
our idea on captive
audiences, like

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.900
our peers in the
department of architecture

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:08.380
or junior high students
from the Cambridge area

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.660
that we volunteered to
quote and quote teach

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:14.260
and then we went and tested our
prototype curricula on them.

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:18.100
And every evening
it's embarrassing,

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:21.790
it's a little hard to put
unfinished, messy work out

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:26.500
into the world and be judged,
but we found that number one,

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:28.840
we gathered a ton of
useful information

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.490
that we were only
going to gather

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:34.040
by engaging with real
people, putting our product

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.790
back to the half baked as
it was in front of them.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.290
And the other thing
that we realized

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:42.970
is that people were incredibly
forgiving and helpful

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:46.150
the moment that you explain this
is something I'm working on,

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:49.780
I value your opinion, or we
were talking to you because you

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:53.560
do have valuable insight and
perspective that we want access

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.690
to, and we think this
project is important

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:59.140
but we can't get there without
feedback from people like you.

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:01.390
The moment you say something
like that people buy

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:02.620
and they're forgiving.

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:06.280
The other thing is a rough
draft and we found that

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:08.530
from junior high school
students all the way up

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:13.180
to PhD candidates in MIT, people
were willing to take their time

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:16.600
and engage with your prototype
and give you earnest feedback.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:21.670
So test early, test often, get
over the kind of shyness of it

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:25.690
and just see what people think
of what you're working on.

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.010
I think that's a
really important point.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:30.580
I think especially for
a lot of creative people

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:33.700
it's sort of the intersection
of passion and vocation,

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:37.600
and that vulnerability can be
really difficult to overcome,

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:41.380
but you've got to get
out of that comfort zone

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:43.570
in order to thrive and
flourish in the way

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:46.220
that you're hoping to succeed.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:50.090
So Ellen same question.

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:54.320
Yeah I think if I was
talking to myself two years

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:57.350
ago just starting out on
this I would remind myself

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:01.250
that no matter the outcome
of the competition,

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:04.580
you're going to learn more about
yourself than maybe even you

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:06.950
learn about your
venture in this process,

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:12.840
and that is a worthy
venture if you will.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:16.640
I also think that it's
an incredible opportunity

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:19.280
to get outside of
the walls of MIT,

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:24.860
a place that can often be
very claustrophobic and inward

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:25.850
looking.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:28.400
We used it as an
opportunity to finally get

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:30.470
to know neighbors that
we never interacted

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:32.930
with when we spent
all of our time

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:35.660
in building seven and studios.

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:38.390
And so I would use
it as an opportunity

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:40.580
to get to know yourself
better as a human,

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:44.330
as somebody who's going to
be joining the workforce,

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:46.610
as somebody who
works with others

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.250
no matter what happens on stage.

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:53.360
It is an incredible opportunity
to get to know yourself

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:55.610
a little bit better too.

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:58.130
I really appreciate that
answer because we're really

00:21:58.130 --> 00:22:01.280
trying to facilitate
edification learning

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.860
betterment for
everybody at all stages,

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:07.100
and it shouldn't
be end game driven.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:09.560
I think the focus and
the priority which

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:12.620
is becoming such a rich part
of the MIT Entrepreneurship

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:17.690
ecosystem is that there is
a spirit of inquisitiveness

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:20.510
and learning, and
that regardless of who

00:22:20.510 --> 00:22:22.610
gets the great big check
at the end of the day,

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:24.650
ultimately there
should be value added

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:27.030
and mentorship for everybody.

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:29.900
So yes, absolutely.

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:33.372
Nir, your turn.

00:22:33.372 --> 00:22:35.330
I'll use some of the
things that you mentioned.

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:39.380
I mean, for me the trick
again is not what's important.

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:42.360
Entrepreneurship it's a mindset
and it's a way of living.

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:45.350
Whether if you will succeed
this competition or no,

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.882
it's tools that will serve
you later on in life.

00:22:47.882 --> 00:22:49.340
And the other part
I'd say that you

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:52.070
need to start at least
nine companies until you

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:53.330
succeed with the 10th.

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:57.930
So it's a long game
entrepreneurship.

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:01.970
Having said that it's super
important to use speed.

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:03.980
You need to make
as many iterations

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:07.250
as you can with very
limited time that you have.

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:11.170
In Creatives, I really
love to work with Creative.

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:13.580
There is one challenge
often is that they're

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:16.340
so passionate about what
they're doing, they're so

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:18.950
focus on the product, the
presentation of the service

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.390
that they forget there's
a customer outside.

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:23.920
So it's important to
have a great product

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.100
but make sure that you
speak to your customers.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.040
And when you go
to your customers,

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:30.620
don't search for the
answer you want to hear.

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:33.350
Come open minded,
be active observer

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:36.170
in order to be able
to really understand

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:37.490
what they are looking for.

00:23:37.490 --> 00:23:38.990
Sometimes they
don't know what they

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:40.530
want until you show it to them.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:44.710
So that's my takeaway.

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:45.760
I really like that.

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:48.010
The insights that
everybody seems

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:50.890
to be sharing today
center around network,

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:54.430
partnerships, willingness to
fail, willingness to learn.

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:59.240
So John, do you have any
other follow ups for that?

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:02.600
Yeah, one of the things
I'll say is always keep

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:08.180
a really tight pulse on
socioeconomic political

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:09.000
situations.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:11.480
And I mean in this case,
I think that for myself

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:15.320
and a lot of entrepreneurs
that I know COVID-19 quickly

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:17.090
had us pivot, but we
were in a situation

00:24:17.090 --> 00:24:19.340
where we were kind of already
able to do that and some

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:23.420
of the technology projects
that we were working on for two

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:25.820
years out from now,
we ended up being

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:27.260
able to look at
those technologies

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.770
and find uses for
them now, where

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:35.930
they could provide assistance
and help in the education realm

00:24:35.930 --> 00:24:38.070
and in some various other areas.

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:40.610
So yeah, having a
very strong pulse

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:44.420
on the adoption of
technology obviously

00:24:44.420 --> 00:24:47.240
is really important,
but especially just

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:49.580
what's going on in the world
where are the anxieties?

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:51.890
Where are the problems that
can be solved and trying

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:55.520
to find that problem
that is relatively new

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:59.010
and doesn't have solutions yet
for it, but we can imagine,

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:02.210
will be in place for at
least the next decade.

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:05.540
That's what I would
really try to focus on.

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:08.060
I think it's very important
what you just said.

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:11.420
Things like ongoing
viability and sustainability.

00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:13.220
It touches back
on what Ellen said

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:16.730
about thinking about
transcending the bounds of MIT.

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:20.090
When you're a member of
the big bad real world,

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:22.940
you still want your venture
to sustain and thrive

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:26.210
and having that mindset, that
forward thinking mindset,

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:29.060
I think is essential
toward ongoing success,

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:31.070
toward forging
deeper partnerships,

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:34.950
getting more buy in and
building your longevity.

